# T09.bd - T09: the ceiling. Does work above 100% vanish,
# or is there hidden tick debt?
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# PURPOSE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# get_cpu() returns 0-100. The question is what happens to work that
# would have pushed it PAST 100.
#
#   CLAMP model .... the meter tops out at 100 and everything above it is
#                    thrown away. A 48-line burst and a 10-line burst are
#                    indistinguishable once both have saturated, and the
#                    decay back down starts from the same place for both.
#   DEBT model ..... an accumulator keeps counting past the ceiling and
#                    get_cpu() only SHOWS min(100, v). A bigger burst then
#                    leaves more residue behind and takes longer to drain.
#
# This matters far beyond bookkeeping. If the meter censors above the
# ceiling, then every in-script CPU guard in this codebase - throttle(),
# cpu_guard_fast(), every "while get_cpu() > threshold" loop - is reading
# a variable that physically cannot distinguish "just barely saturated"
# from "massively over". A guard would read a comfortable number right up
# to the instant the killer fires.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE BURST IS THE EXPERIMENT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Normally an unpaced run of more than ~10 lines is treated as fatal and
# never written on purpose. Here the unpaced burst IS the independent
# variable, so it is deliberately unpaced and deliberately long. Four
# phases run bursts of 10, 25, 40 and 48 straight-line "x = 1" statements
# with no calls, no pacing, and nothing else between them.
#
# 48 is chosen on purpose. The documented kill rule is 100% for 5 straight
# seconds; at the base tier's 10 ticks/sec that is 50 executed lines. With
# the 2 instrument ticks that immediately precede each burst (see PHASE
# SHAPE) the largest phase is exactly 50 executed ticks back to back - the
# sharpest possible probe of the literal rule, sitting right on the line.
#
# A KILL IS A RESULT, NOT A FAILURE. Phases ascend 10 -> 25 -> 40 -> 48 so
# the risky burst is last and every smaller phase's data is already banked
# and printed before it runs.
#
# NOTE: nothing in the corpus has ever measured the lethal UNROLLED burst
# size. The famous "fatal 50-line burst" (test_cpu_decay.bd) was 50 loop
# ITERATIONS - condition + body + increment, roughly 150 executed lines.
# The true straight-line threshold has never been bracketed.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# PHASE SHAPE (identical in all four phases; only burst length varies)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#   log("T09 begin B=n")   <- marker, printed BEFORE the settle so its own
#                             tick has drained away before the burst starts
#   sleep(1) x3            <- settle back to the floor
#   cb = get_cpu()         <- BASELINE, immediately pre-burst  [control]
#   ta = get_unix_time()
#   <n lines of x = 1>     <- THE BURST, unpaced, call-free
#   tb = get_unix_time()
#   c0 = get_cpu()         <- PEAK capture, delay 0
#   sleep(1) x2
#   c2 = get_cpu()         <- RESIDUE capture, delay 2s
#   sleep(1)
#   log(all four numbers)  <- logged only after a sleep, so the log's own
#                             cost can never contaminate what it reports
#
# Three readings do three different jobs:
#   cb  proves the four phases all START from the same state. If cb climbs
#       phase to phase, the phases are not independent and any trend in c2
#       is contaminated by carryover rather than caused by burst size.
#       This is the control the whole test rests on.
#   c0  answers the CEILING half: is the reading pinned at 100 regardless
#       of burst size, or does it scale with burst size?
#   c2  answers the DEBT half: does a bigger burst leave more behind?
#
# burst_s = tb - ta additionally gives four (lines, seconds) points. Those
# calibrate this machine's real ticks/sec (no API exposes it) and, across
# four different burst sizes, separate a flat per-burst overhead from the
# per-line rate - exactly the unresolved "cost per line + flat ~10t"
# question in field_notes.txt.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# PREDICTIONS - stated in advance
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Observed float noise on get_cpu() is ~1e-7, so ANY difference above
# ~0.001 between two readings is real, not noise.
#
# 1. CLAMP CONFIRMED - all four cb equal, all four c0 equal, all four c2
#    equal. Work above the ceiling is destroyed; 2 seconds after the fact
#    a 48-line burst is indistinguishable from a 10-line burst.
# 2. DEBT CONFIRMED (clamp refuted) - cb flat but c2 rises monotonically
#    with B (e.g. 10 / 25 / 55 / 70). Overshoot survives the ceiling.
#    get_cpu() under-reports during saturation and every guard is blind.
# 3. NOT SATURATING - c0 rises with B and is NOT pinned at 100 for the
#    small bursts (e.g. 30 / 60 / 95 / 100). This refutes field_notes.txt's
#    "almost any command pushes get_cpu() to 60-100" reframing and makes
#    get_cpu() a usable gauge of recent burst size.
# 4. CARRYOVER - cb itself climbs across phases. Debt survives a 3-second
#    settle. Read result 2 as contaminated; this becomes the headline.
# 5. LETHALITY - the script dies during a phase. The last printed line is
#    "T09 begin B=K", naming the killer exactly. The lethal straight-line
#    run is then bracketed in (previous B + 2, K + 2] executed ticks, and
#    the already-printed burst_s values convert that bracket to seconds.
# 6. FILE LENGTH - this file is 334 lines, far longer than any test script
#    run so far (previous longest: 89 lines). Importing is known to cost ticks
#    proportional to a file's WHOLE line count, function bodies included;
#    whether a top-level script pays the same at launch has never been
#    tested. Line 1 captures cl before anything else runs. cl near 0
#    matches the known 47-line-file datum and dismisses the concern. A
#    non-zero cl, or zero output at all, is a brand-new finding with large
#    implications for menu.bd's ~11-module startup.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOW TO RUN
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# After T04 and T08. Fresh session, "ps" and "jobs" both empty.
# Runtime is about 45 seconds per run.
#
# REPEATS: run it 3 times back to back. Two runs settle the ceiling
# comparison; the third is free variance data, and variance is the whole
# point of this programme. If B=48 kills one run and survives another with
# nothing changed in between, that is the central run-to-run mystery
# reproduced in the simplest system anyone has managed to build - by far
# the most important possible outcome here. If a kill happens, run twice
# more to check the SAME B kills each time.
#
# OPTIONAL BUT VALUABLE: watch the live in-game CPU meter during the B=10
# and B=48 bursts and note the peak you see. The UI meter is the only
# channel outside this script, unaffected by the script's own get_cpu()
# calls. If the meter peaks the same for B=10 and B=48 while c0 differs
# (or the reverse), the meter and get_cpu() are not the same quantity -
# something no document states and no test has ever probed.
#
# PASTE BACK: every "T09" line, in order, plus any meter peaks you saw.
# Truncated output is still useful - the last line always names the phase
# that was running when it died.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------

var cl = get_cpu()
var t0 = get_unix_time()
var x = 0
var cb = 0
sleep(1)
var c0 = 0
var c2 = 0
var ta = 0
var tb = 0
log("T09 alive launch_cpu=" + str(cl) + " (file-length control; ~0 = launch is free)")
sleep(1)
sleep(1)

# ---------------- PHASE B=10 ----------------
log("T09 begin B=10")
sleep(1)
sleep(1)
sleep(1)
cb = get_cpu()
ta = get_unix_time()
x = 1
x = 1
x = 1
x = 1
x = 1
x = 1
x = 1
x = 1
x = 1
x = 1
tb = get_unix_time()
c0 = get_cpu()
sleep(1)
sleep(1)
c2 = get_cpu()
sleep(1)
log("T09 B=10 base=" + str(cb) + " peak=" + str(c0) + " res=" + str(c2) + " burst_s=" + str(tb - ta) + " t=" + str(get_unix_time() - t0))

